// loft#825 — a `for` variable that collides with something already bound has a diagnostic
// that names the loop variable and prescribes a cure that works. It has to arrive FIRST,
// ahead of whatever the body then fails on.
//
// The body is written so it would fail on its own: `hs` is a float accumulating `p.0`,
// which is an integer. Reported alone that error names `hs`, a variable that is not the
// problem, and tells the reader to rename it — which reproduces the same error under the
// new name, forever. Renaming the loop variable is the only fix, and it is what the one
// line of output says. `hs` does not appear at all, because the collision is caught on
// pass 1 and the parse stops before the body access it would have caused.
//
// loft#915 narrowed WHICH collision this is. Two sequential loops reusing one name is no
// longer one — each loop binds its own variable, so the second inherits nothing and the
// program compiles. What is left, and what this case now uses, is a loop variable landing
// on a plain function local, where the loop really does clobber a binding the rest of the
// function reads.

fn main() {
  p = 7;

  hb: vector<(integer, text)> = [(11, "x"), (44, "y")];
  hs = 0.0;
  for p in hb { hs = hs + p.0; }

  println("hs={hs} p={p}");
}
